und. This suggests they likely do not have a King (which would make Quads) or a Pocket Pair (which would make a Full House), or they are slow-playing. However, for me to win at showdown, the opponent must have a hand where both cards are 5 or lower (to chop) or lower than 5 (impossible since 2,3,4 are the only options and I hold two of them, leaving few combinations).\nIf the opponent holds *any* card 6 or higher, their kicker plays, and they win (e.g., K-K-K-J-6 vs K-K-K-J-5). It is statistically very probable they have a card higher than 5.\nBluffing is risky because my passive history (checking Flop and Turn) does not credibly represent a King or a Full House. A bluff here is likely to be called by any Ace-high or mid-pair hand. Preserving chips is the priority.\n\nReason: I am playing the board with the lowest possible kickers. The opponent almost certainly beats me with a high card. Bluffing is not credible given the checking history. Checking (0) minimizes loss to just the blinds/ante involved.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
